I'm also not a lawyer, but I've had extensive (unfortunately personal) 
experience with tort law.  It's fundamental to the concept of a tort
that the claimant must not only suffer some sort of damage (i.e., the 
act must be not only illegal but actually harmful in some sense), but
that the damage was foreseeable and some act (or negligence) by the 
accused must have caused it.

There's no imaginable damage to the (erstwhile, former, ex-) DevTeam
if a new DevTeam is formed and published a NetHack 4, or 5, or 12. 
NetHack's license doesn't allow it to be sold, so no one can claim 
lost profits on it.  It wouldn't even cost them their opportunity to
publish their own NetHack 4.0 -- or 3.4.4, or any other name they 
felt like applying.

I looked at JO's postings, but as has been noted above, I'm not very
adept at usenet; I may have missed something that contradicts this. 
But I'm sure of this: to sue a person for injury, there must exist:

1) An injury,
2) An injured party willing (or alive) enough to sue, and
3) A law identifying the injury as something prohibited.

The 3) may exist, although I'm not convinced of it under US civil 
law and I gather JO isn't either.  1) and 2) are completely out, 
though, so the jeopardy seems effectively nil.

